C674x floating-point instruction tests
======================================

gen.py turns the special-case rules of TI's "TMS320C674x DSP CPU and
Instruction Set Reference Guide" (SPRUFE8B, July 2010) into test vectors
for the 30 floating-point instructions (ABS, ADD, SUB, MPY*, CMP*, the
conversions and the RCP/RSQR approximations).  The model works on exact
rationals and uses no host floating point, so it checks the emulation
against the manual and not against softfloat.  main.c runs each
instruction on its own through inline asm and prints every mismatch.

Covered: signed zeros, denormals (read as signed zero, flushed on
output), infinities, quiet and signalling NaNs, NaN_out and its sign,
overflow and underflow, rounding to nearest, integer saturation.

Not covered:
- the rounding modes and the FADCR/FAUCR/FMCR status bits;
- whether underflow is detected before or after rounding, on which the
  manual is silent: such vectors accept both 0 and the smallest normal;
- RCP/RSQR beyond the promised 2^-8, as the exact seed tables are not
  documented;
- deviations of real hardware from the manual.

Building needs a C6000 cross compiler (CROSS_COMPILE, default
tic6x-openadk-uclinux-) and python3.

qemu
----
  make run-qemu QEMU=<build>/qemu-system-c6x

The bare-metal program prints "fails N" and leaves qemu through the
dsk6455 exit port with status 0 or 1.

Real hardware
-------------
The instructions exist on C674x (C6748, OMAP-L138) and C66x (C6678)
cores; the C6455 that qemu's dsk6455 models has no FPU, so the tests
need a different board there.

- Under Linux: "make fpu-linux" builds an ordinary program that only
  needs printf and putchar.
- Bare metal, e.g. loaded over JTAG: point the console at the board's
  16550 UART and leave out the exit port, which only qemu has; the
  program then spins after printing.  For a C6748 with UART2 and DDR:

    make fpu-baremetal.elf BM_DEFS=-DUART_BASE=0x01d0d000 \
        BM_LDFLAGS="-Wl,--defsym,RAM_BASE=0xc0000000 -Wl,--defsym,RAM_SIZE=0x4000000"

  The UART must be set up (clock, baud rate) by whatever loads the
  program.  This configuration has not been tried on hardware.
